  "live_records": {
    "q-342097": {
      "dom_id": "step-q342097",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14218806/assets/781d755a292a18934af5b28436593262070527aa41da3bda092f979d388ef1f7.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)=$  \n{{field-1}},\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)=$  \n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "As $x$ approaches $-2$ from the *left*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $-2$ from the *right*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
