    "q-106429": {
      "dom_id": "step-q106429",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14218806/assets/52478c6cdcae0ba1e46b34eea8a4115f2f5f14d8973604c5ab589842bb7db262.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ (shown above) is defined on $x\\in [-4,1).$ Find the right-sided limit at $x=-4$ and the left-sided limit at $x=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "As $x$ approaches $-4$ from the *right*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $-1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{+}}f(x)=-1\\,.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $1$ from the *left*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=3\\,.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-106429-a",
              "type": "text",
              "value": "${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{+}}f(x)=-1,$ ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$",
