    "q-342137": {
      "dom_id": "step-q342137",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14218806/assets/bd7ee98596021320d0f24d5b813de476ffabaa48156316091e5f23fed9e20d30.png)\n\nThe function $y=g(x)$ (shown above) is defined on $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ Find the left-sided limit at $x=-1$ and the right-sided limit at $x=2.$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}g(x)=$\n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the function $g(x)$ approaches $1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}g(x)=1.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, as $x$ approaches $2$ from the right, the function $g(x)$ approaches $0.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$",
