    "q-106434": {
      "dom_id": "step-q106434",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14218806/assets/f01058e0460ac0de134f36ce8a83aada0a719565f78c640b713526ec1b1a3a99.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?  \n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{3}^{+}}f(x)=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. As $x$ approaches $-3$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=0,$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{3}^{+}}f(x)=0\\,.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ is undefined, Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=-2$, and consequently\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)=-2\\,.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, as $x$ approaches $1$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=0$, and consequently\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=0\\,.\n$$\n\n Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
